# Day 07 – HTTP & HTTPS Traffic Analysis

## Objective
To understand HTTP and HTTPS communication, observe TLS handshake packets, and analyze encrypted application data using Wireshark.

## Environment
- Operating System: Kali Linux
- Packet Analyzer: Wireshark 4.6.6
- Network Interface: eth0
- Client IP: 10.0.2.15

## Tools Used
- Wireshark
- curl
- Kali Linux Terminal

## 1. HTTPS Traffic Analysis

### Command
```bash
curl -I https://example.com
```

### Wireshark Filter
```text
tcp.port == 443
```

### Observations
- HTTPS traffic was captured successfully.
- TCP port 443 was observed.
- TLSv1.3 packets were identified.
- Application Data packets were exchanged between the client and server.
- Encrypted application data was not directly readable in the capture.

## 2. TLS Handshake Analysis

### Wireshark Filter
```text
tls.handshake
```

### Observed Packets
- Client Hello
- Server Hello

### Explanation
Client Hello initiates TLS negotiation by sending supported TLS versions and security parameters.

Server Hello selects compatible TLS parameters for the connection.

TLS establishes encryption keys, allowing subsequent application data to be protected.

## 3. Encrypted Application Data

### Wireshark Filter
```text
tls.app_data
```

### Observations
- TLSv1.3 Application Data packets were visible.
- Client-to-server and server-to-client encrypted traffic was observed.
- Packet lengths and communication metadata remained visible.
- Actual protected application content was not readable.

## 4. HTTP Response Header Analysis

### Command
```bash
curl -I https://example.com
```

### Observed Response
```text
HTTP/2 200
content-type: text/html; charset=utf-8
server: cloudflare
```

### Explanation
- HTTP 200 indicates a successful response.
- Content-Type identifies the response content format.
- Server header identifies the responding server infrastructure.

## 5. HTTP vs HTTPS

| HTTP | HTTPS |
|---|---|
| Application protocol without TLS protection | HTTP secured using TLS |
| Data is not encrypted by HTTP itself | Application data is encrypted in transit |
| Commonly uses port 80 | Commonly uses port 443 |
| Content may be readable in packet capture | Content is generally encrypted |

## 6. Wireshark Filters Practiced

| Filter | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `tcp.port == 443` | HTTPS-related TCP traffic |
| `tls.handshake` | TLS handshake packets |
| `tls.app_data` | TLS application data |
| `tcp.port == 80` | HTTP-related TCP traffic |

## 7. Key Learnings
- Understood the difference between HTTP and HTTPS.
- Learned how TLS Client Hello and Server Hello work.
- Identified TLSv1.3 encrypted application data.
- Understood that HTTPS protects application content while network metadata remains visible.
- Practiced packet filtering and traffic analysis in Wireshark.
- Learned how curl can inspect HTTP response headers.

## Conclusion
Successfully analyzed HTTPS traffic, observed TLS handshake messages, and identified encrypted application data using Wireshark. This practical improved my understanding of secure web communication and network traffic analysis.

